WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.990
I'm Jennifer Pulley, and welcome to NASA Connect, the show that connects you to math, science, technology, and NASA.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:19.670
We're here at the Tech Museum of Innovation in San Jose, California.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.000
Now, you know, this episode of NASA Connect is all about robots in NASA's space program.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.000
Now, you may think of a robot as a mechanical creature that walks around.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.870
But did you know that the first person to use the word robot wasn't a scientist at all?

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:39.770
In fact, he was a Czechoslovakian writer named Karol Čapek.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.250
In Czech, the word "robata" means forced labor.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:48.990
Now, in his play, Rossum's Universal Robots, Čapek used the word to describe electronic

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:52.930
servants who turn on their masters when given emotions.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:01:00.000
In 1941, science fiction writer Isaac Asimov first used the word robotics to describe the

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.640
of robots and predicted

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.660
the rise of a powerful robot

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.820
industry. In the 1950s,

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.540
it seemed like robots were featured

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.360
in nearly every science fiction

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.140
movie and TV show.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:12.690
Like me!

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.250
But since then, robots have moved

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.510
from science fiction to science.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.000
Unimate was the first

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.920
industrial robot used

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.760
in a General Motors automobile factory

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:24.820
in 1961.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.890
Since then, the field of robotics

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.000
has advanced as computers

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.420
computers have become more powerful and compact.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.780
With powerful computers, scientists can program robots with artificial intelligence so that

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.060
they can make decisions.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.660
When I let go, it will straight away start again into the right slinky action.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.660
During the course of this program, you will be asked several inquiry-based questions.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.480
After the questions appear on the screen, your teacher will pause the program to allow

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.970
you time to answer and discuss the questions.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.000
This is your time to explore

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.830
and become critical thinkers.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.170
But before we take a look at all the different types

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.310
of robots, here are some questions for you

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.710
to think about and discuss.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:11.470
If you could have your own personal robot,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.980
what would you like your robot to do?

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.300
How intelligent would your robot have to be?

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.990
How would you communicate with your robot?

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.710
Teachers, pause the program, and students,

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.060
write down what you think.
